+# ADR-005: Flywheel Design Principles and Elegance Standard
+
+**Kind**: Foundational vision
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Deciders**: Flywheel Core Team
+
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Flywheel is a Bun + TypeScript + SolidJS + Effect + OpenTUI application with a local-first session model and an owned agent runtime hosted in its own process, which every client reaches over the runtime protocol. The codebase has four durable source layers plus a CLI composition root:
+
+```text
+infra <- workflows <- orchestration <- tui
+cli imports from all layers as the composition root
+```
+
+SolidJS is a TUI-layer technology. Every Solid import lives under `src/tui/`, where stores, signals, memos, effects, and component ownership turn typed runtime snapshots and patches into OpenTUI rendering. Orchestration owns plain typed records with revision counters and a coalesced notifier; the TUI reconstructs UI-readable state from protocol data rather than sharing reactive object identity with the runtime. Reactive identity never crosses the production runtime boundary.
+
+Effect is a first-class runtime discipline here, not a helper library. Its scope is async lifecycle, scoped resources, structured concurrency, typed runtime errors, streams, PubSub, and Schema. Effect does not own business logic, durable state, or TUI reactivity; those boundaries remain plain TypeScript domain code, SQLite facts/projections, and Solid inside the TUI. Flywheel runs on Effect v4 without turning the application into an Effect container.
+
+This ADR is Flywheel's canonical, project-specific definition of elegance and the audit rubric used to judge the codebase. Elegant Flywheel code works with the grain of its tools, makes ownership and causality obvious, contains no duplicated authority or speculative ceremony, and leaves each line doing load-bearing work for the intended user experience. A reader should be able to explain why every boundary and abstraction exists from the behavior or invariant it protects.
+
+`AGENTS.md` operationalizes this standard for coding agents. The overlap is deliberate: this ADR is the durable human-readable audit authority, while `AGENTS.md` is the instruction surface applied during work. Neither may be slimmed by silently dropping a rule; any rule removed from this ADR must first remain fully represented in `AGENTS.md`, and any disagreement must be resolved in both documents.
+
+---
+
+## Core Thesis
+
+**Work with the grain of your tools.**
+
+- Bun's grain is "the runtime does it."
+- Solid's grain is fine-grained reactivity.
+- TypeScript's grain is structural inference.
+- Effect's grain is scoped async lifecycle.
+
+If a pattern feels ceremonious, the idiomatic solution is usually simpler.
+
+## Tool Divisions
+
+Each first-class tool owns one architectural job:
+
+| Tool | Owns | Does not own |
+|---|---|---|
+| Bun | Runtime, subprocesses, file APIs, tests, build scripts | Wrapper layers that only rename Bun or Node APIs |
+| TypeScript | Domain modeling, discriminated unions, structural inference, factory DI contracts | Runtime validation of external data without a schema |
+| SolidJS | UI-readable reactive state, memos, stores, component ownership, OpenTUI rendering reactivity | Async resource lifecycle, provider stream cancellation, typed runtime error channels |
+| Effect | Scoped async lifecycle, resource acquisition/release, structured concurrency, `Fiber` interruption, `Deferred`, typed errors, Effect-native streams/PubSub/Schema where adopted | Business logic, XState transition policy, session state authority, Solid's reactive graph |
+| OpenTUI | Terminal rendering primitives, input surfaces, display constraints | Domain state, persistence, workflow control, session policy, agent runtime policy |
+
+Effect is first-class for lifecycle and typed async boundaries; Solid remains first-class for TUI reactivity; TypeScript remains first-class for concise domain structure; Bun remains first-class for runtime primitives.
+
+## Core Motto
+
+Maximize elegance, not minimal churn. Prefer the design that leaves the codebase simpler, more symmetric, and easier to extend, even when that requires a larger refactor now.
+
+---
+
+## Layer Boundaries
+
+```text
+src/cli/
+  Composition root. Wires auth, config, registration roots, headless mode,
+  and the TUI launcher. No domain logic.
+
+src/infra/
+  Shared foundation: events, paths, output blocks, logging, formatting,
+  auth/LLM primitives, atomic writes, redaction, parsing. Imports nothing
+  from src/. No Solid, no rendering.
+
+src/workflows/
+  Workflow domain: definitions, the compiler, pure XState control and
+  verification, typed workflow facts, the concrete-attempt projection,
+  bounded attempt execution, handoffs, artifacts, and worker-prompt assembly
+  (attempt-input assembly; per-turn provider-request assembly lives in the
+  runtime, ADR-007). Imports infra only. No sessions, config loading, agent
+  runtime creation, or rendering.
+
+src/orchestration/
+  Application runtime: config, sessions, session store, chat/workflow
+  controllers, agent runtime, runner contexts, persistence wiring,
+  budget/transcript resources. Uses plain typed state and Effect
+  lifecycle services. No Solid, JSX, or OpenTUI rendering.
+
+src/tui/
+  Terminal presentation and input: shell, hooks, keyboard handling,
+  command dispatch, modals, output components, theme, adapters. Reads
+  session views and calls orchestration actions. No workflow-view/session/agent
+  domain logic.
+```
+
+Run `bun run check:boundaries` after any file addition or move.
+
+Narrow interfaces at boundaries. Pass the smallest thing that satisfies the consumer: `EventBus` instead of a whole session, `updateEntry` instead of a store object, `WorkflowViewStore` instead of a filesystem path convention.
+
+No forwarding-address modules. When ownership changes, move the code and update imports directly.
+
+---
+
+## Decision Checklist
+
+Run these before planning or implementing a change:
+
+| # | Check | If violated |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Is there exactly one source of truth for this data? | Use the existing fact, projection, domain record, TUI store, or workflow-view field. Do not copy it. |
+| 2 | Store or event bus? | UI-readable state enters the TUI store through protocol data. Independent infrastructure notifications go to the bus. |
+| 3 | Memo or effect? | Derived value -> memo. I/O -> effect. Signal-setting inside an effect that reads another signal usually wants a memo. |
+| 4 | Async lifecycle or reactive state? | Resource lifecycle -> Effect. UI-readable state -> Solid store/signal. |
+| 5 | Does this belong in orchestration or TUI? | Business logic -> orchestration. Rendering/JSX -> TUI. |
+| 6 | Does this belong in workflows or orchestration? | XState workflow policy, facts, attempts, and projections -> workflows. Config, sessions, agent runtime, and production wiring -> orchestration. |
+| 7 | Can TypeScript infer this? | Annotate module boundaries. Delete interior annotations the compiler does not need. |
+| 8 | Is this abstraction used in 2+ places? | If not, inline it unless SRP would be harmed. |
+| 9 | Is disposal ordered and awaited? | Finalize, flush, dispose. Fire-and-forget loses data. |
+| 10 | Would deleting this code change behavior? | If not, delete it. |
+| 11 | Internal or external boundary? | External input -> accept recoverable variation, normalize once, then publish a typed internal shape or a clear typed error. We wrote the data -> reader throws on malformed input, writer enforces the invariant. |
+
+---
+
+## State Ownership
+
+One source of truth per value.
+
+- Workflow progress and terminal state live in committed `workflow:<workflowId>` facts. Workflow-view rows and workflow state are a deterministic concrete-attempt projection of that control.
+- The non-session workflow aggregate owns run configuration and the current parent invocation binding. Session creation owns immutable workflow membership; reverse indexes are projections.
+- Accumulated workflow context is a projection of `context.*` companion facts in the `workflow:<workflowId>` aggregate.
+- Each session contains one agent conversation, backed by the SQLite `conversation:<conversationId>` tree projection. Each started workflow attempt receives its own session directly under the launching chat.
+- Session lifecycle and relationships live in the SQLite session metadata projection.
+- Rendered output history is derived from the owning domain facts at read time. Agent history folds the session's conversation and execution facts. A workflow has a card, not a transcript. Both clients use their ordinary child-session body for workflow agents; workflow presentation supplies only header and navigation. Live output blocks are a runtime projection, never durable history authority.
+- Session content has one byte owner: the content-addressed `tool_content` store, keyed by session id and sha256. Ordinary tool output above the inline threshold uses `{ sha256, bytes, preview }` references in tool facts and chat entries. Its content rows commit in the same atomic writer-lane item as their referencing facts. The writer proves each reference at append; the conversation fold, tool projections, nested-fact hydration seam, and FTS insert verify exact text at hydration. Ordinary text read pages carry bytes between those proof boundaries, and the live bus keeps inline text.
+- Large display evidence uses immutable bounded chunks and manifest pages in that same store, not indivisible whole-patch rows. The writer stages children before parents and proves the complete hash-and-length closure before a small settlement can name its root. Per-file settlement owns applied evidence independently of the final tool result. File lists and patch bytes cross boundaries through bounded pages and byte windows; a single long line spans chunks. Tool results, checkpoints, and notices carry bounded metadata and references, never another copy of the patch.
+- Chunked-role content nodes live for the session lifetime, including unsuccessful stages and indirectly referenced children. Ordinary tool-text pruning remains unchanged and excludes those nodes. Requested derived net patches use the same store: distinct bytes add session-lifetime storage, identical bytes deduplicate, and derived display never authorizes Undo. `clear_session` removes staged, applied, and derived content atomically with the deletion fact. No second byte owner, lifecycle store, or periodic tree collector exists.
+- Skill and memory mutations remain domain-owned writes with their own journals. Only their display artifacts use the shared content reference primitive and reader. They do not publish project file-mutation facts or enter Session Changes or Undo.
+- Each conversation owns one typed checkpoint in session facts. Its latest state remains current across conversation rewind, and the fact log retains every version for inspection. Only the owning agent writes its checkpoint. Compaction records the current checkpoint and recall instructions together as the recorded, model-visible opening message of the fresh context; the transcript shows only the compaction report. Legacy Markdown files under `scratch/notes/<conversationIdHex>/` are first-use conversion input for Working notes, not another authority. Other scratch artifacts remain disposable.
+- Active runtime projection lives in the session store.
+- Request usage belongs once to the performing session or workflow auxiliary aggregate. Workflow policy excludes nested subagents; display includes descendants once. Historical unallocated usage remains an explicit residual. Workflow fairness is a separate admission key, never an accounting owner.
+- Typed execution resources belong to actual agent sessions. Workflow bookkeeping and work-owner controls belong to the run. Imported resource provenance preserves recorded scratch and note paths without copying files.
+For each project context, installed skill packages live in one published registry revision assembled for that project root. One immutable source inventory declares Flywheel's reusable agent-method root and workflow-method root with their architectural kinds. The revision retains the complete bundled corpus and derives the production core corpus, its sorted package-identity manifest, the model catalog, the TUI slash catalog, and every composed prompt section. No consumer maintains its own package list or body copy, and source-root kind never participates in package identity.
+Account-level definition reconciliation has one authority for each datum. Override membership is positional: a non-bundled definition is an override exactly when it sits at the catalog path of a default in the bundled default catalog, and a definition with no bundled counterpart is the user's own and is never reconciled. The strict sidecar manifest at `<userDataRoot>/definition-override-baselines.json` is the sole authority for the acknowledged-default identity of each physical project or global override. The bundled default catalog is the sole authority for the defaults shipped by the running build. Override files carry no identity metadata; every identity is a digest of exact file bytes. Override-adjacent snapshots are rebase evidence, and at first observation an existing snapshot's identity seeds the acknowledged baseline before the manifest becomes the authority. The manifest is machine-owned and the snapshots can rebuild it, so a manifest that cannot be read never stops a project from opening: its bytes move aside next to it with a warning, and every override is observed again as at first observation. Conflict and staleness status are derived by comparing the manifest's acknowledged hash with the bundled catalog's current hash; no status field is persisted or synchronized.
+Canonical access-provider candidate order is a fixed pure function over canonical spend mode, numeric provider rank, and captured account order. One admitted request owns the ordered walk. Each candidate retains its grouped exact-wire model offerings, and each exact route is selected at most once; in-place transient retries and same-account login recovery retain that selection. Before provider work starts, Flywheel selects ordinary standard routes when present, otherwise contributor routes only when the captured active-profile policy allows training routes. An exact-account preference can instead place a contribution-declared free standard route before its ordinary standard sibling without changing candidate order. A failover-class failure on that free route advances to the ordinary sibling before the next candidate and does not by itself publish account quota or provider outage state. A settled output, authentication rejection, provider rejection, malformed request, or unknown failure never advances to a sibling. Offering preference never reorders candidates, so an allowed earlier subscription contributor route remains ahead of a later API standard route. Captured authentication conditions, incompatibility, authorization, availability, quota, proven unaffordability, and a route that cannot run on this machine, such as a provider whose local program is missing or too old, can skip an exact candidate. After provider work starts, only the named failover class for spent quota, throttling, overload, or provider-stated outage advances the walk after settlement. Authentication rejection waits only through the interactive external-login condition described under Secret Handling; otherwise it terminates under its own classification. Provider rejection, malformed requests, and unknown provider failures terminate under their own classifications. None invents quota or service observations. Accounts with included usage expand into `included`, `prepaid`, and `automatic` candidates. An external account that its engine explicitly classifies as usage-only has one `direct_api` candidate with subscription account identity; direct APIs use `direct_api`, and aggregators use `aggregator`. Spend classes always order included value before prepaid balance, automatic spend, direct API spend, and aggregator spend. Full request and routing identity includes spend mode, while one named canonical account identity intentionally owns credentials, model catalogs, and eligibility across that account's modes. Catalog and declaration order are non-semantic. Availability reads current normalized profile observations without polling providers or copying credit state. Included mode requires no paid-use consent. ADR-020 owns the additional evidence-and-consent admission required for prepaid and automatic subscription candidates; direct API and aggregator credentials authorize their paid-use paths by presence, and explicitly classified usage-only external accounts use normal metered billing without entering that authority. A denied or unreviewed subscription envelope skips only its exact mode, does not create retry or suppression state, and lets the fixed candidate walk continue. A profile-local ceiling is policy, not consent: it can hold pooled Copilot seats below their provider allowance, never enables paid use, and never changes candidate order. ChatGPT rate headers affect included mode only. Availability v8 keys each exact account-mode row by the effective auth profile identity and candidate identity in its own partition; provider outage rows remain provider-scoped and global across profiles, so neither profile-local account state nor provider-wide service state can alias the other. ADR-017's profile-scoped protocol-v6 Usage authority keeps provider observations separate from its immutable Flywheel request ledger. Each provider metric has a stable key, explicit scope, and exact-or-unavailable timing; current account quota totals report every containing exact account-wide window independently and include only source-local ledger entries inside that window's half-open bounds. Shared request settlement records the exact serving candidate, opaque auth-owned credential lineage distinct from canonical account identity, token counts, and price evidence for every served attempt. The profile ledger commits before the atomic session usage and budget fact mirror. Provider refresh never rewrites request accounting, provider-observation history and ledger ranges remain separate, and provider invoices remain authoritative for actual charges.
+
+Downstream representations are projections. A value that exists in one authority file or store field should not also live as a shadow signal, registry property, adapter field, or duplicated status enum.
+
+Derived state is advanced, not recomputed. A consumer that runs per change, per tick, per live subscription, or per member of a loop reads the state that its owner already keeps live (the session store, the summary index, a registered projection) instead of rereading durable history, and its cost per change is bounded by the change, never by the number of members or the length of history. A fold reads its aggregate once and then only the facts after its last position. Rereading or refolding whole history per change, or reading each member durably per change, turns linear work into quadratic work that grows with every agent and every fact, and it competes with commits for the same state lane. Full reads belong only to one-shot paths: an explicit person command, a migration pass, recovery, and startup.
+
+`bun run lint:projections` provides partial syntactic enforcement, not complete coverage. Source-text patterns check projection construction and unfed `getState()` reads. The aggregate-read check uses the shared tree-sitter loader on TypeScript and TSX under `src/`. It flags unbounded `readSlice`, `capturedFacts`, and `readCapturedFacts` queries outside a syntactic `heldProjection` ancestor, plus `read` and `readTolerant` queries whose visible keys all belong to `FactQuery`. It follows object literals, lexical `const` bindings, spreads, conditional alternatives, and TypeScript expression wrappers. Every visible alternative must have a defined `afterPosition`, `fromSeq`, `positions`, `limit`, `toolUseIds`, or `parentToolUseIds` bound; an upper bound alone does not count. It also flags those reads and the durable-request methods listed in `scripts/aggregate-reads.ts`, including `readPage` and `capturePosition`, in same-function `for...of`, `for...in`, and listed array-iteration callbacks. The reviewed ledger records acceptable sites and their actual caller cadence; entries with no matching finding fail.
+
+The lint does not follow helper calls, imported queries, mutable query objects, aliased or computed method names, or receiver types. It trusts unresolved queries and spreads and opaque bound values. It excludes counted `for` and `while` loops as paging or retry syntax. It cannot prove a bound advances, a caller is one-shot, or a syntactic `heldProjection` ancestor owns a read. Projection-registration and freshness patterns also miss aliases, helpers, and arbitrary formatting. Call tracing and work-count tests cover these blind spots. A change to a hot path ships with a work-count test that counts state requests per change through `tests/helpers/state-requests.ts`. The release gate counts the state requests that the fifty-agent workflow launch costs per launched member and fails when the count grows past its paired ratio to the pinned baseline. It gates the count, not the rate, because a saturated state lane holds the rate near its capacity and spends extra requests as a longer launch.
+
+New per-session runtime state is a typed fact plus a projection that reads it. The session directory contains the ephemeral `owner.pid` process lock and the disposable `scratch/` workspace; neither is persisted state. Legacy Markdown files under `scratch/notes/` remain available only as conversion input for the typed checkpoint. Chat trees, checkpoints, workflow handoffs, workflow view, context, and metadata are typed events in `.flywheel/flywheel.sqlite` (ADR-001). To add per-session state, add a fact type and a projection. Diagnostics that no projection replays are debug-log records in `.flywheel/log/*.jsonl`, never facts and never files under the session directory.
+
+Temporary side-question exchanges are an explicit runtime-only exception. One session-scoped module host owns their bounded history and publishes its view to clients; clients do not keep another conversational authority. Closing their surface can cancel a request but never interrupts the parent. Session-host disposal ends the temporary history. Request usage and inspection remain durable, and only an explicit Keep action creates a normal saved chat.
+
+TUI stores should reflect domain slices, not component trees. An orchestration session-store entry is a plain runtime record; the TUI store is its protocol-fed client projection.
+
+Inside the TUI, use `createStore` + `produce` for coherent state domains and `createSignal` for isolated leaf values. Do not use `{ current: T }` ref-bags for shared state; model the state as an object with named methods or a proper owner field.
+
+### Reactive Dependency Ownership
+
+A Solid effect's tracked reads are its input contract. Effects track causes, not the state they reconcile. An effect that writes a signal or store must not also track that target, or helper reads whose values can change because of the write. Capture the canonical source publication in the tracked phase, then run reconciliation inside `untrack`; use `on` when naming the source set makes the contract clearer. Use a memo instead when the result is pure derivation.
+
+Fresh object identity is not new semantic authority. Runtime snapshots and patches may publish a new object whose meaning is unchanged. Reconciliation must be idempotent, and a target write must never feed back into the effect that consumed the source publication.
+
+Reactive regression tests establish the effect before they exercise the active view, then publish a fresh semantically equivalent source value and assert bounded execution, correct state, cleanup, and continued input. Tests for Solid effects run with the `browser` export condition under a real reactive owner; Bun's default server export can leave effects inert, and a static render is not proof of live propagation. User-visible TUI behavior also requires installed-binary UAT after the asynchronous publication that can trigger the effect.
+
+---
+
+## Postel Boundary Discipline
+
+Be liberal at external boundaries and conservative inside Flywheel.
+
+External systems are allowed to be messy. User-authored config, provider streams, LLM tool-call arguments, network responses, SDK callbacks, and CLI subprocess output may arrive partial, loose, reordered, over-specified, under-specified, or shaped by provider quirks. Boundary adapters should accept recoverable variation, normalize it into Flywheel's canonical typed shape, and produce clear typed errors only when the input is ambiguous, unsafe, or cannot be repaired without inventing meaning.
+
+Once data crosses into Flywheel-owned state, Postel's law stops for current writes and post-recovery execution. Historical facts are a reviewed recovery boundary because the enforcing older binary no longer exists: decode strictly first, apply only family-owned salvage rules, then represent unreadable bytes as typed degraded gaps. One canonical recovered history powers transcript display and provider API context. This tolerance does not authorize guessed executable state, relaxed current producers, or redispatch of recorded tool results.
+
+Unreadable bytes remain immutable. A session-local decode or migration failure never blocks workspace startup, another session, or later appends. Migrations isolate failures per session and never rewrite or delete bytes they cannot parse. A project-wide SQLite or project-directory open failure remains a startup failure because no session boundary exists below it.
+
+Project notification history is the deliberate disposable exception. Before runtime readers open the store, the notification owner folds the complete aggregate through the current strict schema. If any read, schema, or projection invariant fails, it deletes the complete `project:notifications` aggregate and its sequence authority in one transaction, then starts from an empty projection. It does not repair a row, publish a partial projection, or preserve sibling notifications. Notifications carry no executable control or user work, so malformed notification history never blocks runtime startup.
+
+Those two remedies are not interchangeable. Inside a state generation this release still reads, a fact shape may be upgraded in place in two sanctioned forms. The first is semantic adoption: a named pass over a known staged fact shape this workspace owns, which decodes every candidate against the current schema, rewrites the physical representation and appends the typed fact that states the semantic adoption inside one transaction, runs before any runtime reader opens the store, and refuses the whole workspace rather than completing in part. The appended fact is the semantic authority; the rewrite is representation only. The second is the operator-invoked tool-content dedup pass, which rewrites historical inline tool text into content rows plus references, one session per transaction, idempotent and resumable; both shapes are valid current schema versions, so the pass adopts no semantics, appends nothing, and a store whose operator never runs it stays fully readable. Neither exception buys reader tolerance: no fallback default, no ad hoc repair inside replay, no branch on a shape that no registered schema version or appended fact states, and no semantic rewrite that leaves no recorded evidence.
+
+Session metadata replay preserves every loadable session as ordinary metadata. Current creation facts record `executionCwd`; replayed metadata may omit it. At each path-sensitive use, one resolver selects an existing recorded `executionCwd` or `projectRoot` without writing a fact or changing metadata. The resolved scalar drives execution and path rendering.
+Retirement deletes only a session for which strict metadata replay reports an error, no tombstone exists, no recoverable session identity exists, and no metadata fact is newer than this binary. Every false conjunction preserves exact event bytes. Temporary sessions, valid v1 and v2 sessions, and intact identity-only sessions remain listed. An invalid-JSON creation with no recoverable identity retires. Newer-version fences are evaluated in one batched query for all retirement candidates.
+
+Filesystem artifact deletion precedes the SQLite retirement transaction. These resources are not cross-resource atomic: a crash can leave database rows after artifact removal, and the idempotent next startup retries retirement. The architecture does not claim atomic publication across the filesystem and SQLite.
+
+A declared incompatible generation whose epoch this release can read is retired instead, and the project starts an empty current generation in its place. Retirement is declared, never inferred: a release states the disposition of every generation it knows, and state whose generation is undeclared, corrupt, or unreadable fails closed, keeping its exact layout while the start refuses. An epoch above the current one can only come from a later release, so its refusal is a named start refusal that every client shows with the update as its remedy, never the lane reset. A generation is replaced only while no other process owns it, and the epoch that licenses the replacement is read from the database again at each destructive boundary: once before its members are moved aside, and once before the first of them is deleted, so nothing is moved or discarded on the word of a manifest alone. Retirement takes the generation whole, so a project's disposable session artifacts go with its durable facts and nothing that survives claims authority over what was removed. The bytes a retirement holds aside are temporary crash-recovery state that lets an interrupted replacement finish; they are not an archive, no reader or decision consults them, and they are deleted once the replacement is published.
+
+Durable user state outlives the binary that wrote it. Credentials, model preferences, saved provider catalogs, configuration keys, and model and provider identifiers are the user's, and a release that changes the shape of any of them converts the previous shape in the same unit: one conversion beside the store that owns the file, run before any reader opens it, idempotent under the owning lock, carrying the date after which it is removed, and covered by a test that feeds the previous shape. Strictness inside Flywheel is about current writers and readers agreeing; it never licenses a reader to treat a shape it does not know as empty. A reader that meets an unknown shape says so in a warning and publishes nothing it did not read. Installing a newer release never requires a person to log in again, reselect a model, or repair a file by hand. (This is distinct from ADR-004's within-run contract versioning, which governs a single run, and from the state-generation retirement above, which is declared per generation and never inferred from a failed parse.)
+
+True external boundaries -- user TOML, LLM and tool output, provider stream events, network responses, SDK callbacks, CLI subprocess output -- must normalize permissively and reject clearly. Internal replay/projection/runtime paths must treat malformed input as an `InvariantViolation` and throw unless the owning domain explicitly declares the complete state disposable. The workspace-wide session metadata catalog isolates a malformed session into a per-row error so one bad fact cannot hide other sessions. Project notification startup recovery retires its complete malformed aggregate because notifications are disposable and non-executable.
+
+### Where tolerance belongs
+
+| Boundary | Tolerance? | Rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| User TOML config | Limited | Accept only unambiguous ergonomic variation; reject unknown, ambiguous, or unsafe settings with actionable errors. Deliberate exception: a legacy `roles.*.provider` key is ignored with a one-line warning naming the key, because access-provider routing is automatic and rejection would brick a previously valid config. |
+| LLM tool-call arguments and streaming JSON | Yes | Partial-parse, buffer, repair known model syntax damage, retry, or ask for restatement when semantics are recoverable; never invent missing intent. |
+| Provider stream deltas, usage metadata, cache markers, and response envelopes | Yes | Accept provider quirks and extra fields; normalize into the internal LLM contract before emitting runtime events. |
+| External SDK and network responses | Yes | Accept over-specified or version-skewed shapes; publish narrow internal types or a typed boundary error when required semantics are missing. |
+| CLI subprocess output | Yes | Parse defensively, preserve raw evidence where useful, and normalize only stable semantics into facts or tool results. |
+| SQLite facts, projections, workflow view, chat tree, handoffs | None | Throw `InvariantViolation`; fix the writer. |
+| State generation classification | None | Read the generation's declared disposition strictly. An undeclared or unreadable generation fails closed: refuse the start with nothing on disk touched, rather than guessing which state may be deleted. |
+| Session metadata catalog | Isolate | The catalog fold captures malformed session metadata as a per-row error instead of throwing, so one corrupt fact cannot unlist the workspace. Retirement applies only when the exact predicate above is true. |
+| Project notification history | Discard | Fold the complete aggregate strictly before runtime readers open it. Any read or projection failure deletes the complete aggregate and its sequence authority transactionally, logs one warning, and starts empty. Notification history never blocks startup. |
+| Workflow handoff facts | None after publication | Validate the worker capture before recording `handoff.written`; projections parse the typed fact and throw on mismatch. |
+| Internal IPC and tool input built by our runtime | None | We wrote it; producer bugs should fail loudly. |
+| Auth, font, and model caches | Convert, then bounded | A previous shape this release knows is converted in place before the first read. Refetch on a genuinely unreadable cache is acceptable when the cache is cheap to rebuild, but log loudly; never silently publish empty state, and publish an account whose discovery has not been attempted as pending, never as failed. |
+| Availability authority (`<userDataRoot>/availability-authority-v<protocol>.sqlite`) | None | We wrote every row: decode current-schema rows strictly and throw a typed corruption error. The file name carries the protocol version, so a bump moves coordination to a new file and strands no existing binary. A file at this path that states another version anyway belongs to a foreign writer and fails closed rather than being guessed at, because uncoordinated requests would spend an account another binary believes it is metering. Nothing quarantines, renames, or rebuilds the file: other processes hold it, and an authority that replaces itself under them is two authorities. A process that fails closed keeps its own observations and commits none. Contention, permission, and I/O results are transient and cost only that observation's durability. |
+
+### Normalizing at external boundaries
+
+- Tolerance belongs in the boundary adapter, not downstream consumers. The adapter may accept provider quirks, extra fields, partial chunks, recoverable syntax damage, and version skew; the object it publishes is Flywheel-owned and must satisfy the internal contract.
+- Preserve raw evidence when it helps audit, replay classification, or debugging, but do not let raw external shape become an alternate source of truth.
+- Repair only syntax, framing, ordering, or representation errors whose intended semantics are unambiguous. If a boundary would need to invent missing intent, fabricate a default, or choose between meanings, reject with a clear typed error or route through an explicit retry/clarification path.
+- When sending data to external systems, emit the narrowest complete documented shape we can. Do not rely on the receiver's tolerance to make malformed Flywheel output work.
+
+### Enforcing at the writer
+
+- One `effect/Schema` is the writer's gate. `parseFact` before `store.append`. The brief's compiled output contract decodes each submission at the handoff tool, with exact keys at every level, before `handoff.written` is recorded. `validateEntry` runs before chat-tree append. The reader may re-validate, but a successful write is the source of truth.
+- Strict schemas. No `.passthrough()`. No `.default()` on a field the writer always sets -- a schema default is a contract that the producer is allowed to omit the field.
+- SQL-level constraints. `NOT NULL`, `CHECK`, `UNIQUE`, and foreign keys turn writer bugs into write failures instead of read-time surprises.
+- State-machine gates reject illegal writes. Raw XState verifies legal workflow events before the host can build and append a proposal; the reader never repairs an illegal transition.
+- Cross-event invariants live with the writer. `file.mutation.settled` requires a matching `file.mutation.prepared`. The projection does not paper over a mismatch.
+
+### Failing at the reader
+
+- Projections that hit malformed state throw `InvariantViolation` with event position, fact type, and a producer hint. Existing patterns to copy: `parseProjectionFact` and the workflow-view projection in `src/workflows/view/`. The session metadata catalog captures a failure as per-session error state so one corrupt session cannot unlist the workspace. Project notification startup recovery instead retires the complete disposable aggregate before any runtime projection opens it.
+- No `flatMap(x => x ? [x] : [])` on internal data. No `?? default` on a field the writer always sets. No `try { ... } catch { return [] }` on our own SQLite.
+- `safeParse` on internal data is a design smell. Use `parse` and let it throw. `safeParse` belongs at external boundaries where normalization, repair, rejection, and retry paths are part of the contract.
+
+This rule reinforces the substrate direction set by ADR-001 and the replay honesty rule set by ADR-001: a substrate that silently swallows malformed facts cannot honestly support replay, fork, or diff.
+
+---
+
+## Secret Handling
+
+Secrets -- API keys, OAuth access/refresh tokens, credentials -- are `Redacted.Redacted` in memory everywhere. Wrap at the earliest resolution point (env var, auth store, credential helper); unwrap (`Redacted.value`) only where the raw value is genuinely needed: the adapter making the provider call, or one explicit serialization boundary.
+
+The runtime identity key that presents an account to the relay, the Web Push signing key, and a live pairing secret are secrets under the same rule: redacted in memory, unwrapped only inside the signing closure or the serialization boundary of the pairing store.
+
+A boundary that must serialize plaintext, such as the on-disk auth store, gets an explicit plain DTO with a `to*`/`from*` conversion pair mirroring the disk `Credential` shape. The crossing is intentional and typed; everything else stays Redacted, so an accidental log, snapshot, error object, or `JSON.stringify` renders `<redacted>` rather than the key. The structured logger also runs a defense-in-depth pattern scrub, but the type wrapper is the primary guard. No secret in errors, traces, logs, or snapshots.
+
+An external login keeps its secrets outside Flywheel. The program that owns the login, such as the Claude CLI, runs the sign-in, stores the token in its own store, and refreshes it. Flywheel never reads, stores, copies, logs, or sends that token, and never reads the program's credential store or keychain item. It reads only the program's token-free account record, which names the account, and it passes a code that a person pastes straight to the program's standard input without keeping it. The runtime credential of an external login is the location of the program's login, which is not a secret.
+
+An external provider owns each project's in-memory account login condition, its opaque question identity, its single accepted answer, and its abortable request waiters. An interactive request whose external login is refused waits in the provider walk after its execution settles, outside stream deadlines. Successful same-account login renews the exact saved request on the same candidate with fresh execution resources; it does not prepare another request or advance failover. No, close, failed or cancelled login, and different-account login preserve the original failure and decline the condition until successful login or a served request clears it. Stop removes only its waiter at once. Login waiting does not count as child inactivity or extend a bounded failure-summary deadline. Headless tasks and stdin/stdout chat retain immediate authentication failure. Auth views and questions derive from this condition; notices never own its answer.
+
+A module configuration section enters request evidence only through its explicit `requestCapture` projection. The owner decodes its section and selects nonsensitive JSON values; the request boundary clones and freezes those values once. Whole module section bags never enter request evidence or the client protocol. A section without a projection contributes no request snapshot.
+
+---
+
+## Retry and Idempotency
+
+A retried attempt or agent turn is a resume that replays committed state, never a blind re-run. The event-sourced conversation (the chat tree, ADR-008) is the idempotency ledger: a tool call whose result is committed sits in the replayed history, so the model continues past it instead of re-executing. Do not add a parallel idempotency key, dedup record, or attempt-level "resumed" marker -- that is a second source of truth for what already ran, when the chat tree already records it at tool-call granularity.
+
+The same ledger makes a person's message idempotent. The runtime records a message under the key that the client's submission identity gives it, and a copy that the client sends again after its connection lost the answer finds that key in the conversation, in a held cell, or in a workflow's request addendum, and records nothing (ADR-010). The key is the entry id, so no separate dedup record exists.
+
+Provider fallback has one owner: the admitted request's ordered walk. Adapters perform one wire attempt each time they are asked, and outer runners do not retry provider failures. The walk retries a candidate in place, with bounded exponential backoff or the provider's declared backoff, when that candidate throttles, saturates, or loses the connection, and it reports each retry so the session shows the reconnect while it waits. It advances to the next candidate only when those retries are spent. A spent quota advances at once, and so does a route that cannot run on this machine, such as a local program that cannot start, because another start meets the same machine and nothing reached the provider to mark. Completed work and observed usage settle before every attempt, retry and advance alike. A durable outage mark rests on the spent candidate's last failure, and only a failure the provider stated qualifies: an HTTP status, a declared backoff, or a backend error frame. A lost connection never marks, because it can equally be this machine's doing. A first mark holds one minute; a recheck that fails again doubles the hold up to five minutes, a recovery ends the escalation, and a mark that lands inside a standing hold restates it without lengthening it. Cancellation and local safety or durability failures stop the request; terminal provider failure requires exhausted candidates. Explicit model reassignment remains a separate runner transition.
+
+The only unsafe window is a tool call that executed but whose result was not committed before the crash. It is reconciled -- never re-executed -- by synthesizing a tombstoned result (`interruptedToolResult` / `healConversation`), so the model sees an interrupted/no-result signal and decides. A non-idempotent external effect (a destructive bash command, a network write) that landed in that window cannot be auto-recovered; the honest design surfaces the uncertainty to the agent rather than promising an exactly-once it cannot deliver.
+
+---
+
+## Reactive Architecture
+
+| Primitive | Role | Rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| `createStore` / `createSignal` | TUI source of truth | Written by the local TUI owner from typed snapshots, patches, or input. One canonical client location per datum. |
+| `createMemo` | Derived state | Pure function of reactive inputs. Never manually sync derived state. |
+| `createEffect` | I/O boundary | Persistence, external services, subscriptions, DOM/OpenTUI side effects. No business logic. |
+| `batch()` | Coalesced writes | Use around bursts of related writes so Solid publishes one coherent update. |
+| `createRoot` | TUI ownership scope | Required for TUI reactive ownership outside component trees. Dispose it with its TUI owner. |
+
+Only the TUI uses Solid. Infra, workflows, orchestration, and CLI composition remain plain TypeScript except for their owned Effect lifecycle boundaries.
+
+Avoid reactive diamonds: if a memo reads both an original signal and a store that an effect derives from that signal, it can observe stale intermediate states. Derive directly from the original source or collapse the dependency path.
+
+---
+
+## Event Bus vs Store
+
+The event bus is for cross-cutting infrastructure and lifecycle notifications:
+
+- budget tracking
+- stream observers
+- workflow-view lifecycle events
+- output projection inputs
+
+The TUI's Solid store is for client state the UI reads, fed by typed runtime snapshots and patches:
+
+- output blocks
+- attempt rows
+- tokens/cost/context percentage
+- model phase and runner activity
+- connection state
+- active runner projection
+
+Callbacks are allowed for 1:1 lifecycle signals such as `onRunnerDone`, `onRunnerError`, or a single manager `onChange` subscriber. Do not build callback chains that only forward state from A to B to C.
+
+`FlywheelEvent` is a discriminated union in `src/infra/events.ts`. Event names use lowercase dot-delimited domain and action segments, such as `agent.run.started`. Producers emit raw events; subscribers filter.
+
+---
+
+## Session Lifecycle
+
+Persisted session lifecycle has exactly two states:
+
+```text
+active <-> inactive
+```
+
+`active` means this process owns or expects a live runner. `inactive` means no live runner exists; the session is history or resumable disk state.
+
+Do not add session states for workflow completion, failure, pause, deletion, archive, or trash:
+
+- Workflow terminal state lives in committed `workflow:<workflowId>` control; workflow state is its operator-facing projection.
+- Conversation history lives in the SQLite `conversation:<conversationId>` tree projection.
+- Deletion is an action that appends `session.deleted` and removes session artifacts.
+- Parent/child links live in session metadata facts.
+- Child workflow supervision is an in-memory session-store projection.
+
+The `SessionManager` is the authority for persisted lifecycle and relationships. The session store is the active runner pool and runtime projection. ADR-010's app service projects both through typed snapshots and ordered patches; the TUI reads its protocol-fed client stores and never reaches into `SessionManager`, the session store, or SQLite directly.
+
+---
+
+## Definition and Registration
+
+New behavior should register with the owning system instead of expanding switches in unrelated layers:
+
+- Workflow files load by content or path through the chat agent's workflow tool, not through a definition catalog. `src/workflows/definition-registry.ts` owns skills and personas. Each brief is inline. Another record reuses it through `runs`, which names the phase or agent that states it. A brief composes skills by canonical id through `dependencies`.
+- A launched workflow declares each output contract inline beside `brief:` with `output`. File verification also reads JSON contract paths relative to the workflow file. `src/workflows/output-contract/` compiles each contract once. No registry, name, or named check joins a brief to its contract. A module that consumes an agent's output selects agents by the contract's `schemaSha256`. Its template and consumer must agree on that hash.
+- Module tools, projections, reviews, maintenance, and command handlers live in `src/orchestration/modules/<id>/`; kernel tools remain in `src/orchestration/agent-runtime/tools/`. The runtime composes their declared contributions.
+- Commands execute through the runtime command registry. Clients render their declared metadata and submit the registered command identity.
+- Module fact schemas, retained history readers, and historical migrations register with the complete installed catalog. Current selection controls new work, not the ability to decode durable history. The kernel owns migration ordering and workspace exclusion; each module owns its transformations.
+- Typed host seams carry behavior and the minimum authority it needs. A consumer never reaches into another optional module's store, review controller, or runtime implementation. Shared algorithms belong in the lowest lawful neutral layer.
+- Reusable agent methods are `SKILL.md` packages under `src/agent-skills/`; workflow-owned methods are packages under `src/workflows/skills/`. One source inventory admits both roots into one flat registry.
+
+Definitions are source data. Runtime workflow authority is the `workflow:<workflowId>` fact aggregate; XState control and workflow-view rows are reconstructed views. Do not turn YAML, Markdown, workflow-view rows, or output blocks into alternate authorities.
+
+---
+
+## Skill Packages
+
+Durable instruction splits into a kernel and packages. The kernel is an ordered `AGENTS.md` stack: Flywheel loads the user's `~/.agents/AGENTS.md` baseline first, then repository instructions from the repository root through the working directory. Later, more specific files take precedence. Each section keeps its global or repository-relative source label. Instruction files are regular UTF-8 files read without following a symlink; `@file` text stays literal and cannot pull another file into provider-bound context. The stack has one aggregate size cap that drops lower-precedence sections first. These are the rules every turn needs, whatever the work is. Method for one class of work is a skill package advertised by its trigger and loaded on demand (ADR-002). Reusable agent methods live under `src/agent-skills/`; methods whose value depends on workflow semantics live under `src/workflows/skills/`; managed packages live in the project or global roots ADR-011 defines. A rule earns a place in the kernel only when every turn needs it. Everything else is a package, and a package body is never copied back into the kernel, a brief, or a persona.
+
+ADR-011 owns whether learned content is memory or a skill, whether its reach is project or global, and which actors can mutate it. Interactive global skill edits need no separate permission and show their scope in the applied tool result. Package authorship, mutation eligibility, reach, and loading state are separate properties; a `managed` marker never implies scope.
+
+Skills and briefs are separate. A brief belongs to its workflow: it carries the procedure and the output contract, and the agent record that runs it carries the workflow-local policy such as the name override and artifact contracts. An inline brief never enters the skill catalog and is not a slash command. A brief composes reusable skills through `dependencies`. A skill is context that a brief, a persona, the model, or the user loads. A package referenced through another package's `dependencies` is a dependency and is not user-invocable; its consumer composes it by canonical id. A dependency is host-neutral: it states one method and the shape of its own report, and never who dispatches it, how findings are routed or consolidated, or what a workflow synthesizes from them. Those decisions belong to the composing definition, so the same dependency reads correctly in a brief, in a persona, and in an inline chat turn. Among the remaining packages, `managed: flywheel` uses `/learned.<id>` and every other package uses its bare qualified id. Built-in command tokens and the `learned` root are reserved from the skill-id grammar.
+
+Three entry paths reach a package, and all three render the one canonical context that `renderSkillContext` produces:
+
+- **Deterministic composition.** A brief declares `dependencies`, and a persona declares `requiredSkills`. The declared packages compose into the assembled prompt in declaration order, ahead of the definition's own instructions. The declaration is definition data: a brief that names an uninstalled package is a definition error at load, workflow definition identity pins the content hash of every package a referenced brief requires, and a persona that names an uninstalled package fails the persona registry assembly, so it never dispatches a shortened prompt.
+- **Model selection.** The `skill` tool loads and reads the files of any model-invocable package. The model decides, per turn, from the advertised triggers.
+- **User selection.** A published skill slash command resolves a user-invocable package from the live runtime registry and commits the same rendered context as the chat's next turn. Authored package descriptions start with `[Skill]`; Flywheel-managed learning uses `/learned.<id>` and `[Learned Skill]` so the palette and slash suggestions state its origin and narrower role.
+
+No fourth path exists. A consumer that needs package text declares it, loads it, or invokes its user command.
+
+The cross-harness frontmatter keys `user-invocable` and `disable-model-invocation` mean the same thing in every harness that reads the package, so they mark a package that is authored for composition only, everywhere. A package that Flywheel routes to an agent, while another harness without that agent runs it directly, names the persona in Flywheel's own `delegate` key instead: in Flywheel the package is composed by that persona's `requiredSkills` and leaves the chat skill catalog and the slash catalog, the persona registry warns when the named persona does not compose it because the method is then unreachable, and a harness that does not know the key reads an ordinary invocable skill. Publication is the example: Flywheel chat agents dispatch the Ship agent, and peerAgent1 runs the same `ship` package itself.
+
+The catalog is bounded by construction. The prompt advertisement carries every model-invocable package as one capped trigger line, so the advertised catalog is the complete discovery surface and scanning it is meaningful; a new package's description must fit the tighter creation budget so its whole routing signal survives the cap. One more installed package therefore costs one line, and a package earns its place by being loadable on demand rather than by shipping its body in every prompt.
+
+The registry publishes immutable revisions. One watch observes skill roots and the project's and account's persona directories. A saved definition is available without a restart. Workflow folders do not load or enter the watch. Their files remain unchanged, with one warning per folder. A chat can launch a retained file by path. A reload publishes one whole revision, and failure keeps the last complete revision. Each call observes one revision and each catalog or corpus view derives from it once. Complete bundled packages remain available to policies that need all built-ins. Production core membership derives from agent-root kind after development-only filtering. A malformed bundled package is a build bug and throws. A malformed external package that has never loaded is skipped with a warning and does not break startup or freeze reload. Removal requires a successful reading of its root. An unreadable root or highest-precedence package keeps the last complete revision, so an editor's partial write cannot delete a package or expose a shadowed install. Personas follow the same rule.
+
+An external package is untrusted content, and the registry treats it as such at every crossing. A load binds the package to the symlink-free directory it was read from plus that directory's `dev:ino`, and every later byte, the body included, is read through that binding by a descriptor-relative walk: each component is opened relative to its parent descriptor and refuses a symlink, so a directory replaced between two opens cannot redirect a read out of the package. A platform that exposes no descriptor-rooted open serves no filesystem package rather than serving one through a path it cannot trust. The body file is measured before it is read, so a package over its admission limit costs one `fstat` instead of an allocation of its size. What the package then contributes to the prompt is data, not authority: metadata is flattened to one line, so a description cannot open a prompt line of its own; the body is rendered verbatim inside a fence carrying its own sha256, named as third-party content that cannot override system, tool, or user instructions; and provenance names the layer and the package, never a local absolute path, because the rendered context is durable model context.
+
+Children stay lean. A persona declares the packages its lens always needs, so its method is composed before its first turn instead of spent on a load; it carries only its own short role instructions beyond them, and everything else it scans and loads itself.
+
+### External Packages And Contained Support
+
+An admitted external package revision is the single source of truth for its procedure. Flywheel does not vendor, translate, or copy that body into a local skill. When a host runs a package helper, it materializes only the captured, identity-verified bytes under the session scratch directory for that run.
+
+Support execution is ephemeral and contained. Materialization creates no installed registry, durable package copy, or second package authority.
+
+A child grant is an orchestration capability, not a general permission conveyed by skill text. A module contributes a grant through the `skillChildGrants` seam, and the grant names each child package that the `skill` tool may run. Child execution is denied unless the grant names the package.
+
+---
+
+## Harness Minimalism
+
+New built-in harness features must earn their place. Prefer small, observable primitives over opaque convenience systems.
+
+Before adding a new agent-facing feature, ask whether it can be represented as:
+
+- a typed event
+- a workflow-view attempt row
+- a handoff
+- a normal file artifact
+- a CLI tool with documentation read on demand
+- a TUI projection over existing state
+
+Features that hide work, inject context invisibly, create private state outside the durable substrate, or require the model to track extra ceremony work against the rest of these principles. This is not minimalism for its own sake -- it is context control: fewer built-ins means less hidden prompt surface, fewer state machines, cleaner replay, and more obvious lineage.
+
+This rule reinforces the substrate direction set by ADR-001 and the inspectability rule set by ADR-002. Where a new capability cannot be expressed through existing primitives, prefer adding a typed event, projection, or workflow phase before adding a bespoke subsystem.
+
+This is the static rule: build the smallest agent-facing surface that works. ADR-003's "Harness Complexity Must Be Minimal and Load-Bearing" (P9) is the dynamic complement: scaffolding that works today is a hypothesis about current model limits and should be revalidated as those limits change.
+
+---
+
+## TypeScript Discipline
+
+Use inference over annotation. Exported functions and module boundaries get explicit return types; interior variables should usually infer.
+
+Prefer discriminated unions over optional fields. Narrow on `kind`, `type`, or another required discriminator.
+
+Use `effect/Schema` for validation (part of core `effect` in v4; there is no Zod in the codebase). At external boundaries (user config, LLM output, provider streams, network responses), prefer `safeParse` as part of a normalization contract: accept recoverable variation, publish a canonical typed shape, or surface a clear typed rejection. At internal boundaries (anything we ourselves write and read back), prefer `parse` so a producer bug throws instead of degrading silently. Schemas should not carry `.default()` on fields the producer always writes -- the default rewrites the producer contract.
+
+Use `satisfies` when validating object shape without widening.
+
+No `any`, no `Function`, no `// @ts-ignore`. Use `unknown` and narrow.
+
+The strict compiler flags are on and load-bearing: `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `noImplicitOverride`, `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`. Under `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` an indexed access is `T | undefined` -- branch or guard it. `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` is deliberately off: it surfaces ~300 low-signal `{ x?: T }`-vs-`{ x: T | undefined }` errors for negligible bug-catching; revisit only with a specific motivating bug.
+
+Casts and non-null assertions are the exception, not a tool. No `as Type` (other than `as const`) and no `!` without a `// SAFETY:` comment naming the invariant that makes it sound. Branch, parse, or restructure first; a cast is the last resort for an invariant TypeScript genuinely cannot express (a registry-erasure or branding seam).
+
+---
+
+## Interface Design
+
+Use factory functions with explicit dependency injection:
+
+- `createSessionManager(deps)`
+- `createWorkflowRunner(opts)`
+- `createAgentRuntime(deps)`
+- `createWorkflowVmHost(deps)`
+High-level modules depend on narrow abstractions. A wrapper is justified when it enables polymorphism, lifecycle ownership, or test injection. A wrapper that only repeats a Bun or SDK interface is ceremony.
+
+Deep modules should expose simple interfaces. If a consumer needs internal knowledge to use a module correctly, the interface is too shallow.
+
+---
+
+## Bun as Runtime
+
+Bun is the framework:
+
+- `Bun.spawn()` over `child_process`, and only inside a process-launch authority: ordinary production code starts an external process through `spawnOwned`, `runOwnedCommand`, or `openExternalUrl` (ADR-009), while explicitly authorized survival uses only `src/infra/persistent-subprocess.ts`; no caller uses a raw launch primitive of its own
+- `Bun.file()` where it fits
+- Bun test runner through `bun run test`
+- Bun bundler/build scripts
+
+Before adding a dependency, check whether Bun, Solid core, Effect, or the standard library already owns the capability.
+
+---
+
+## Effect Integration
+
+Effect owns async lifecycle:
+
+- `Scope` / `acquireRelease` for resources
+- `Fiber` for structured cancellation
+- `Deferred` for completion signals
+- `Schema.TaggedErrorClass` inside runtime boundaries
+
+Solid owns TUI reactive state:
+
+- stores
+- signals
+- memos
+- effects as I/O boundaries
+
+The systems do not bridge through adapter layers. Effect services may call injected callbacks such as `updateEntry`; they do not own a parallel reactive graph.
+
+Colocate Effect services with their domain owner. `session/session-resources-service.ts` is valid. `src/effect/` is not.
+
+---
+
+## Disposal and Lifecycle
+
+Disposal is ordered and awaited:
+
+```text
+1. session-type-specific cleanup
+2. flush budget, tree, and event writers
+3. dispose file handles, scopes, roots, and logs
+```
+
+Async dispose must be awaited. Session-tree save points, event-writer lanes, and logs are all data-loss boundaries.
+
+Attribution follows publication instead of becoming publication authority. Direct tools report only durably settled mutations from their operation-scoped in-memory accumulator, and their terminal hook offers attribution without awaiting it. Attribution follows the files that settle through the shared file edit publisher, including the declared mutations of a Bash command, which are offered when they land; no shell is held for attribution, and attribution failures never alter a tool result. No attribution state is a durable fact or a second file-lineage authority.
+
+Solid's ownership handles TUI reactive cleanup inside roots. Explicit disposal handles filesystem handles, processes, network streams, and Effect scopes.
+
+---
+
+## TUI Rendering Boundary
+
+The TUI renders derived state and sends actions to orchestration. It must not own XState transitions, workflow fact publication, workflow-view projection, session persistence, runner creation, handoff validation, or tool execution.
+
+Single-line selectable headers should render as one `<text>` element. Compose styled chunks into one `StyledText`; do not render multiple row-flex text children for text that should copy as one line.
+
+Hooks own UI composition and local input state. Domain decisions belong in orchestration or workflows.
+
+---
+
+## Code Quality
+
+### SOLID
+
+We follow SOLID, resolved through this project's constraints.
+
+**Single Responsibility.** Extract when the unit owns non-trivial cohesive logic. Inline trivial forwarding.
+
+**Dependency Inversion.** Use DI when it creates a useful seam for tests, runtime selection, or lifecycle ownership. Do not wrap Bun APIs just to rename them.
+
+**Interface Segregation.** Pass the smallest interface that satisfies the caller.
+
+**Open/Closed.** Prefer extension through registration and discriminated unions. When adding a union member, update exhaustive consumers.
+
+### Conciseness
+
+- No abstraction for a single use unless SRP genuinely applies.
+- No callback chains that just forward.
+- No backward-compatibility shims.
+- Delete dead code immediately.
+- No speculative code -- add a capability when a phase needs it, not before.
+- Built means wired: every export has a non-test consumer in `src/`, and code that compiles but is never reached from the production entry point does not exist. Verify in the live TUI, not only in tests.
+- No mocks or fakes in production code -- wire new code into the real system; mocks live only in tests.
+- Cohesion over line count: split a file when it carries more than one reason to change or forces a reader to hold unrelated concepts (AGENTS.md sets the operational 600-code-line backstop; blank and comment lines do not count).
+- Sparse syntax, dense behavior.
+
+### Comments
+
+Comments explain why a primitive or boundary exists, not what the next line does.
+
+JSDoc the public surface of deep/domain modules and boundary APIs -- the symbols a caller drives through types and hover and should not have to read the body to use. Do not JSDoc-restate a well-named, well-typed signature: this is an application, not a published library, so the test for any comment is "does it add what the signature can't" -- a why, an invariant, a contract, or a safety note.
+
+---
+
+## Testing
+
+Use project scripts:
+
+- `bun run test` after changes
+- `bun run lint` after changes
+- `bun run check:boundaries` after file additions or moves
+
+Unit tests should not call external APIs or spawn heavyweight UI flows. Integration and E2E tests own real subprocesses, I/O, and TUI behavior. New TUI behavior needs live TUI verification.
+
+Tests should cover observable behavior, not implementation details. Each contract has one owner test at the strongest boundary that can observe it; another layer tests the same contract only for a distinct risk that owner cannot reach.
+
+---
+
+## Anti-Patterns
+
+| Pattern | Why it is wrong | Instead |
+|---|---|---|
+| Signal inside an effect that reads another signal | Manual derived-state sync | `createMemo` |
+| `{ current: T }` shared ref-bag | Hidden mutable architecture | Named object with methods or store field |
+| Callback chain A -> B -> C where B forwards | Indirection without ownership | A writes store, C reads store |
+| Optional fields representing a union | Compiler cannot enforce variants | Discriminated union |
+| Business logic inside `createEffect` | Effects are I/O boundaries | Pure function or memo plus effect for I/O |
+| Wrapper that re-exports a Bun API | No depth | Call Bun directly |
+| Interior type annotations the compiler infers | Noise | Delete annotation |
+| Pre-filtering events before emitting | Couples producer to consumers | Emit raw, subscriber filters |
+| A view that rereads or refolds durable history, or reads each member, on every change | Quadratic cost that grows with members and history and starves commits | Read the owner's live state; advance a held fold from its last position |
+| `dispose()` without `await` | Data loss | Await disposal |
+| Store value duplicated in signal/ref/adapter field | Two sources of truth | Read from the authority |
+| `src/effect/` technology namespace | Hides domain ownership | Colocate with owner |
+| `catch(unknown)` at Effect boundary | Loses typed error information | Tagged errors and boundary mapping |
+| Class-level `AbortController` for runtime lifecycle | Unstructured cancellation | `Fiber`; bridge to AbortController only at signal APIs |
+| Memo reads both source and effect-derived store | Reactive diamond/stale intermediate state | Derive from one dependency path |
+| `safeParse` on data we wrote | Tolerance where loudness is required | `parse`; let `InvariantViolation` surface |
+| `?? defaultValue` on a field the writer always sets | Silent corruption past a producer bug | Remove the default; throw on missing |
+| `flatMap(x => x ? [x] : [])` over a projection | Drops malformed rows without notice | Throw on the malformed row |
+| `.default(...)` on a Schema field the writer always sets | Schema lies about the producer contract | Remove the default; require the field |
+| `safeParse` / `?? undefined` on an owned fact in a reader | Softens an owned-data invariant into silent degradation | Decode once via the typed primitive (`parseProjectionFact`); throw on mismatch |
+| Hand-extracting fields from a known fact with `asString`/`asNumber` | Validate-and-discard: the schema exists, the reader re-derives it | Parse the fact into its typed shape once; read typed fields |
+| Parallel idempotency key / dedup record / attempt "resumed" marker | Second source of truth for what already ran | The event-sourced conversation is the ledger; retry replays it |
+| Repair/normalize/legacy branches on internal data | Hides writer drift | Fix the writer; delete the branch |
+| Optional options field guarded by a runtime `X requires Y` throw | The requirement is invisible at every construction site, so the untested path crashes on its first live request | Require the field on the type the constructor accepts; a completion seam produces that type (`RunnableAgentOptions`) |
+| `try { ... } catch { return [] }` reading our own SQLite or session disk | Silent empty state on real corruption | Throw; the substrate is broken |
+| "Backward compatibility" framing for internal schemas | Treats an internal storage shape as a public API | Migrate or invalidate stored state; preserve only user data that would otherwise be materially lost |
+| Provider/model quirks handled after canonicalization | Leaks external mess into internal code | Normalize once at the external boundary |
+| Rejecting recoverable LLM/provider syntax damage before normalization | Treats nondeterministic external systems as if they owed us internal invariants | Repair or retry when semantics are clear; reject when meaning is missing |
+| Package body copied into `AGENTS.md`, a brief, or a persona | Second source of truth for one procedure | Declare `requiredSkills`, or let the model load the package |
+| Dependency package that dispatches, routes findings, or synthesizes for its host | Couples one method to one orchestration, so a second host cannot compose it | The dependency states the method and its own report; the composing definition owns orchestration |
+| New always-loaded instruction block for a case-by-case need | Unbounded prompt growth for context most turns do not use | A skill package advertised by its trigger |
+
+---
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Architecture has one dependency direction and one lifecycle model.
+- Solid is the reactive state system inside the TUI only. The runtime publishes typed snapshots and patches; no reactive object identity crosses the runtime/TUI boundary (ADR-007).
+- Effect is limited to async lifecycle instead of becoming a second application framework.
+- The event bus handles delivery to every subscriber; the store handles UI-readable state.
+- Sessions, workflow facts, workflow-view projections, handoffs, and conversation trees have clear authority boundaries.
+- Agents and future contributors have concrete rules for deciding where code belongs.
+- External nondeterminism is absorbed at adapters; internal state remains strict, replayable, and invariant-preserving.
