+
+The host that starts the guardian also ends that guardian's work only on its proven death, on every platform. The kernel's report that the guardian process exited is the one proof of the guardian's death. At that report the host forgets that guardian and then, for each launch, tells its owner and ends its container. The owner hears the guardian's pid and its exit code or signal. So an owner that decides to end its work for that loss records its decision before the stop that carries it out, and that decision, not the stop, is what names the end. An owner that fails when it is told still has its container ended. The host starts a new guardian at its next launch, adoption, or generation. So executors and tools launch again with no restart of the host. A Worker thread that launches its own containers through a bridge to the host asks the bridge for the guardian's pid at each launch. The bridge answers from the generation it leases into, and when that generation cannot answer because its guardian ended, the bridge opens the next generation in the new guardian. So a Worker's launches recover the same way. The new guardian never adopts the containers of the one that died, because a proven guardian death ends that guardian's work. One guardian runs at a time: a guardian that failed its handshake and exits after a newer one started ends nothing of the newer one.
+
+A hard kill can end the host, the guardian, and every command supervisor at once: `flywheel kill`, a force quit, or an OOM kill. Then no live process sees a death, and the payload keeps running in its process group with no parent. So on macOS and Linux the guardian keeps a durable copy of its leases, the ledger. Before it answers an adoption, the guardian writes one record for the lease in this user's private root `flywheel-leases-<uid>` in the temporary directory. The record names the container by its pid and the identity that the adoption proved, and names the guardian by pid, start stamp, and boot. Each record is a file of its own guardian, named by the token and the guardian's pid, because a handover leases one command under one token in two builds' guardians at once. Every end of the lease removes the guardian's own record and never another guardian's: a release, and a generation close or a sweep that proves the container empty. When the guardian is already gone, the host's release of a container that it proved empty or handed over removes that guardian's record instead. A container that a sweep could not empty keeps its record. The records only have to survive processes, not the host, so no write is synced. A record that cannot be written is reported once, and the lease stands without it.
+
+After its handshake answer, and never before it, each guardian reads the records whose guardian is proven gone in this boot, and removes each record of another boot without acting on it. The container's supervisor then decides what happens. A supervisor that runs with the recorded identity watches its guardian and ends its container itself, or it serves the handover that a later owner adopts it through. So the guardian checks such a record again every 250 milliseconds while it serves. When its channel ends, the guardian starts no further pass, sweeps its own leases beside the recovery pass in progress, and waits for that pass at most the kill grace, so the host's exit deadline covers its exit. The guardian process then exits, and a record that the pass did not finish stays for the next guardian. When the guardian has adopted the command since, it removes the dead guardian's record, and its own record of the command stays. A container whose supervisor is proven gone but that still holds a process is an orphan. The guardian records that decision once at INFO, with the container and the dead guardian, and ends the orphan as it ends its own leases: it proves the container's standing before each signal, and it reaches marked processes outside the group. A container that is proven gone, or whose pid now names another container, loses its record. On Linux the kernel shows a user the environment of each process of that user, except a program that cannot be dumped or that runs set-user-ID, such as `sudo`. So for a group whose leader is gone, the guardian reads the environments of the members that have not exited. When each member shows an environment without the launch's mark, the group is not the launch's, because a process that took the pid after the group emptied leads it. The guardian then leaves that group and the children of its members alone, and ends only the launch's marked processes outside the group. When none is left, it records that decision once at INFO and re