+
+Every process fact an ownership decision rests on comes from the kernel, and no liveness, identity, or membership decision starts a program. Linux reads `/proc`, including the parent in each process's `stat` and the environment in its `environ`. macOS reads libproc through `bun:ffi`: `proc_pidinfo` with `PROC_PIDT_SHORTBSDINFO` answers a pid's process group and status, `proc_pidinfo` with `PROC_PIDUNIQIDENTIFIERINFO` answers the kernel's unique id for the process, `proc_listpgrppids` answers a group's members, `proc_listallpids` and `proc_listchildpids` answer every process and a process's children, and `sysctl` with `KERN_PROCARGS2` answers the environment a process started with. Both `proc_pidinfo` calls answer for a process of any user. Windows asks kernel32. A process identity is a value a recycled pid never matches: on macOS the pid, the process group, and the unique id, which the kernel never gives to another process within one boot; on Linux the pid, the process group, and the start time in clock ticks; on Windows the pid and the creation time. Every such fact has three answers: proven absent, proven present with an identity that matches or proves different, and no answer. Only an absence or a different identity changes ownership. No answer never ends or releases work and never reads as an absence; an admission that must be decided at once refuses with the true reason, and the next admission asks again. No answer is still recorded. A watch or a retry loop records its first unanswered check of a streak once, with its evidence, and counts the repeats in one record when its window closes or the streak ends. So a supervisor's guardian watch records an identity probe that does not answer, and an answer in between does not start a new streak. A supervisor, or the launcher or adopting owner of a command whose supervisor died, that empties a container records an escalation that did not prove the container empty, and a guardian records a sweep that did not prove every container empty, with the containers it left. A process that cannot open a kernel process API records that once, and every probe that rests on the API then answers unproven. A launch that cannot read the guardian's identity refuses because the guardian could not be identified, never because it is not running, and a guardian that cannot read an adoption's identity refuses it as unproven, never as not a container. A watch keeps its current state and asks again on its next check. A process that exited and waits to be reaped is absent: it runs nothing, holds nothing, and a signal reaches nothing. A live process at a container's pid that does not lead its own group proves that container gone, because the kernel never gives a pid to a new process while a group with that id exists. A signal-zero refusal is a process that exists. A library that cannot be loaded answers nothing and never reports an absence. A Windows job that is opened by name is absent only when the open fails with `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND`. An open that fails for any other reason, such as a refused access right, is no answer, so the guardian refuses that adoption as unproven. The host's process watchdog reads its parent through the same kernel surface on macOS and Linux. On Windows the watchdog uses signal zero only.
+
+A durable record that names a process uses the same kernel facts. The records are the ownership locks, the reclaim claims, the shared holders, the session owner records, and the guardian's lease records. Each stores the pid, the kernel's start stamp, and, on macOS and Linux, the boot that the stamp counts from. The start stamp is the libproc unique id on macOS, the `/proc` start time on Linux, and the creation time on Windows. The boot is `kern.bootsessionuuid` on macOS and `boot_id` on Linux. Windows stores no boot, because a creation time is absolute. A record's identity never includes the process group. A record's holder is gone only when its pid holds no process, holds a process with another start stamp, or the record names another boot. Each of these keeps the holder: no answer from the kernel, a host boot that cannot be read, a record that cannot be read, and an identity version that this build does not know. A record without an identity is held while its pid runs and is never taken over. An earlier release writes that shape, and so does a process that cannot read its own identity: it still takes its locks and writes its records, logs one warning with the reason, and asks for its identity again at its next record, keeping only an identity that it could read. It is never refused a lock for that reason; it loses only the protection against a reused pid. Only a holder that is gone is reclaimed, swept, or recovered. Every lock, reclaim claim, and shared holder is published whole through an owner file and a link, so no current writer leaves an empty claim or holder. The owner file is named after the lock or gate that the record belongs to, outside the names that any build sweeps for claims or shared holders, so no build removes it before its link. A session owner record is written whole to a pending file and renamed into place. A claim whose claimant is gone is removed under a claim of its own.
+
+Raw platform launch is confined to seven modules: `src/infra/owned-subprocess-backend.ts`, `src/infra/process-container.ts`, `src/infra/owned-command-supervisor.ts`, `src/infra/process-supervisor.ts`, the dedicated persistent authority `src/infra/persistent-subprocess.ts`, the bounded Windows ACL authority `src/infra/windows-private-directory-acl.ts`, and the bounded terminal-successor authority `src/infra/foreground-successor.ts`. Every other production file reaches a process through an ordinary owned entry point or the persistent-process manager. `bun run lint:owned-processes` rejects any other direct or aliased Bun spawn, Node child-process API, Deno command, shell wrapper, dynamic import, `globalThis` launch, or raw backend import.
+The terminal-successor authority starts one process, the build a terminal follows where the platform cannot replace a process image (ADR-010). That successor must share the shell's foreground job and read and write the terminal itself, which a container in its own process group with piped streams cannot, and the terminal that starts it only waits for its status. It is not a general process-launch surface.
+The Windows private-directory ACL authority reads the current account SID from the process token's `TokenUser` field. Every private-root admission reads its current kernel DACL and protection flag, including admission of an existing directory. Only an exact protected DACL granting that account inheritable full control needs no mutation; every other root receives that DACL through native security APIs. A native failure refuses admission before private data is written. The authority caches only the immutable account ACL, never a directory admission. Its separate read-only verifier uses bounded `whoami` and `icacls` calls to inspect grants. Those commands never mutate permissions and are not a general process-launch surface.
+
+On macOS and Linux, ending a command reaches processes that left the adopted group with `setsid`, `set -m`, or a double fork. Each POSIX launch sets `FLYWHEEL_CONTAINER` to its ownership token. Every process the command starts inherits the mark, and the kernel keeps the environment each process started with. A matching mark therefore identifies command membership, independent of the process group. When the kernel withholds that environment, a process belongs to the command while its parent belongs to the command or its group. A nested owned launch replaces the ordinary mark with its own token. Strict launches also retain every strict ancestor token in process-boundary metadata. An outer end therefore settles nested detached work even after its immediate owner exits. A persistent process or guardian starts without its starter's mark or strict context. A persistent supervisor carries its own mark for its explicit stop contract. Before each signal, the ending boundary reads the process table and signals the group. It also signals marked processes outside the group whose start stamps still match the first observation. These processes hold the container until the kernel proves them gone. An unanswered start probe keeps ownership.
+
+Default natural payload exit settles the group alone, so an intentionally detached server keeps running. A guarded launch can opt into `onPayloadExit: "end-descendants"` instead. Its natural exit uses the marked ending boundary and retries unattended until settlement, with the original payload status. The launcher's fallback also settles that boundary if the supervisor crashes. `enableStrictDescendantSettlement()` establishes this requirement for a process's subsequent guarded launches. Nested guarded launches inherit it even with explicit environment objects. Omission of the per-launch option cannot weaken the inherited requirement. Supervisor argv and existing diagnostic metadata record the selected policy without new steady-state records. Persistent lifetime, guardian survival, and default Windows job, breakaway, and console behavior stay unchanged.
+
+Two ways out remain: a process that clears its environment, and a macOS platform program whose parent exited before the command ended. A service manager such as `launchd` or `systemd` starts its jobs outside the command too. Hostile commands require an external sandbox or operating-system containment boundary. Flywheel does not claim that the mark contains a command that hides from it.
