+- A command supervisor's diagnostic records never enter a shell command's output; the process that launched the supervisor relays them to its own log.
+- POSIX container escalation sends TERM through the process group before any KILL, and KILL only to proven members. A stop, an end, a guardian's sweep, and strict natural-exit settlement also signal processes outside the group with a matching mark or strict ancestor token, or withheld environments whose parent belongs to the command. Default natural exit settles the group alone.
+- Ordinary ownership ends with its owning scope while the platform container remains addressable; hostile escape containment requires an external sandbox.
+- Background launcher scopes fail closed unless their reduced OS priority is established and verified before payload start; nested scopes inherit the requirement, while normal control and state scopes omit it.
+- Persistent execution is OFF by default, requires persisted or CLI host authorization plus per-call opt-in, is managed only for the CLI host lifetime, and releases without termination at the final host boundary. POSIX persistence launches and stops through the shared supervisor's persistent lifetime and retained control fd, never through PID identity probing or host-side process-group signaling.
+- A persistent spool records its launcher and its writer by pid and start identity. It is removed only when every recorded holder is proven gone: by the launcher that reaps the writer after its release, or by the next persistent launch's background sweep. A spool whose holders cannot be proven gone stays.
